 "exercises": [
  {
   "id": "a1",
   "source_id": "eq:Eq7",
   "kind": "exercise",
   "bloom": "Apply",
   "place": {
    "at": "end"
   },
   "cite": "van-der-waals-equation",
   "prompt": "<p>Find the pressure of 1.00 mol of CH₄ in 0.250 L at 300 K (a) from the van der Waals equation with Table 16.2.1's constants and (b) from the ideal gas law. ($\\kR = 0.08314$ L bar K⁻¹ mol⁻¹.)</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "multi",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "parts": [
     {
      "part": "a",
      "value": 83.8,
      "unit": "bar",
      "hint": "Work out the two terms of Equation 16.2.1 separately."
     },
     {
      "part": "b",
      "value": 99.8,
      "unit": "bar"
     }
    ],
    "solution": "<p>(a) The repulsive term is $\\knmol\\kR\\kT/(\\kV - \\knmol\\kbvdw) = (0.08314)(300)/(0.250 - 0.04278) = 24.94/0.2072 = 120.4$ bar, and the attraction takes off $\\ka\\knmol^2/\\kV^2 = 2.283/(0.250)^2 = 36.5$ bar, so $\\kP = 83.8$ bar. (b) $\\kP = \\knmol\\kR\\kT/\\kV = 24.94/0.250 = 99.8$ bar. The excluded volume alone would raise the pressure above the ideal value by 20.6 bar, but the attraction lowers it by 36.5 bar, so at this density the attraction wins and methane pushes less than an ideal gas would.</p>"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "a2",
   "source_id": "fig:16.2.1",
   "kind": "exercise",
   "bloom": "Apply",
   "place": {
    "at": "end"
   },
   "cite": "van-der-waals-equation",
   "prompt": "<p>From $\\kbvdw = \\frac{2}{3}\\pi\\ksigmahs^3N_0$ and Table 16.2.1's $\\kbvdw = 0.03913$ L/mol for N₂, find the hard-sphere diameter of N₂ in ångströms. ($N_0 = 6.022\\times10^{23}$ mol⁻¹.)</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "number",
    "value": 3.14,
    "unit": "Å",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "hint": "Convert $\\kbvdw$ to m³/mol before solving for $\\ksigmahs^3$.",
    "solution": "<p>$\\kbvdw = 0.03913$ L/mol $= 3.913\\times10^{-5}$ m³/mol, so $\\ksigmahs^3 = 3\\kbvdw/(2\\pi N_0) = (1.174\\times10^{-4})/(3.784\\times10^{24}) = 3.10\\times10^{-29}$ m³ and $\\ksigmahs = 3.14\\times10^{-10}$ m $= 3.14$ Å.</p>"
   }
  }
